---
layout: layouts/base.njk
permalink: /atlas/
title: Atlas
description: Schematic maps and running timetables for 21 US rail networks, generated from each agency’s published GTFS feed.
---
{% from "map-embed.njk" import links %}
<section class="wrap">
  <header class="essay-header">
    <h1>Atlas</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      {{ networks.totals.networks }} rail networks,
      {{ networks.totals.stations | thousands }} stations and
      {{ networks.totals.trips | thousands }} scheduled trips. Every map here
      was generated by the same code from the agency&rsquo;s own published
      schedule, with no per-city drawing and no manual correction.
    </p>
  </header>
  <p>
    Each network links to a running map: a real service day, at whatever speed
    you want to watch it. Los Angeles and Mexico City come first, because they
    are the two the essay argues from. The rest follow from the cleanest result
    to the roughest &mdash; the networks the pipeline placed every stop and
    traced every trip on, then those it had to fudge, with the caveats under
    each one saying exactly how.
  </p>
</section>

<ul class="atlas">
  {% for n in networks.networks %}
    <li id="{{ n.key }}">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>{{ n.name }}</h2>
        <p class="meta">
          {{ n.stations }} stations ·
          {{ n.lines | length }} lines ·
          {{ n.trips | thousands }} trips on {{ n.date | formatDate }}
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="thumb" href="{{ ('/maps/' + n.animation) | url }}"
         aria-label="Open the running map for {{ n.name }}">
        {{ n.svg | inlineMap | safe }}
      </a>
      <div class="wrap">
        {{ links(n) }}
        {% if n.notes.length or n.caveats.length %}
          <ul class="caveats">
            {% for c in n.notes %}<li>{{ c }}</li>{% endfor %}
            {% for c in n.caveats %}<li>{{ c }}</li>{% endfor %}
          </ul>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>

<section class="wrap">
  <h2>On the imperfections</h2>
  <p>
    Where a network above lists caveats, they are real, and I have left them
    visible rather than quietly dropping the affected trains. Most come from the
    same two places: an agency numbering its platforms in a way the schematiser
    merges differently than the timetable expects, or a network dense enough
    that some station names have nowhere to sit without overlapping another.
  </p>
  <p>
    Miami is the weakest result here, and the reason is interesting — Metrorail
    and Metromover are almost separate systems sharing one feed. Washington DC
    is missing entirely, because WMATA publishes its GTFS only behind an API
    key.
  </p>
</section>
